   =20
 =20
  <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;"><strong>A few things worth knowing:</strong>=
</p>
 =20

   =20
 =20
  <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">A simple classification does not need a fron=
tier model. A nuanced analysis does not belong on a small one. The router h=
andles the call without you writing any branching logic.</p>
 =20

   =20
 =20
  <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">Roughly 63 percent of auto traffic routes to=
 models priced under $1 per million tokens. That is how simple prompts stop=
 hitting expensive models without any routing code on your side, and it is =
the reason most teams see lower per-request cost on auto than on a single p=
inned frontier model.</p>
 =20

   =20
 =20
  <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">You can still pass <code style=3D"background=
: #f4f4f5; color: #111827; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px; font-famil=
y: 'SF Mono', 'Fira Code', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 14px;" clas=
s=3D"or-inline-code">route=3D&#34;fallback&#34;</code> alongside auto if yo=
u want a named backstop on top of automatic selection. If the auto pick fai=
ls, the fallback list runs in order until one returns a response.</p>
 =20

   =20
 =20
  <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">The auto pool changes as new models launch. =
You do not have to update your code when it does. Read <code style=3D"backg=
round: #f4f4f5; color: #111827; padding: 2px 6px; border-radius: 4px; font-=
family: 'SF Mono', 'Fira Code', 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 14px;"=
 class=3D"or-inline-code">response.model</code> in the response if you want=
 to see what the router picked on any given call, which is useful for loggi=
ng and for spotting cost drift.</p>
 =20

   =20
 =20
  <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;"><strong>When not to use it.</strong> If you =
already know the exact model you want for a given call, pin the model strin=
g directly. Auto is for the 80 percent of traffic where the choice does not=
 matter, not the 20 percent where it does. Most apps end up using both.</p>
 =20

   =20
 =20
  <p style=3D"margin: 16px 0;">The next email covers what changes when you =
add credits to your account.</p>
